; allocator_size_past_the_maximum: a size one past the maximum allocator
; size raises InvalidAllocation on both implementations, under a budget
; large enough that the charge is not what decides it.
;
; spec/xqvm/SPEC.md's "Allocator validation order" (Allocation budget
; section, QUI-1315) resolves an allocator's size operand in a fixed
; order: reject a negative size, charge the budget for it, then reject a
; size past MAX_ALLOCATION_SIZE = 2^32 - 1 = 4294967295, and only then
; narrow it to the target's native width. Step 3 sits after the charge on
; purpose, so under any budget below 32 GiB a size past the maximum
; exhausts the budget at step 2 first and the fault is
; MemoryLimitExceeded -- unchanged behaviour. Step 3 is reachable only
; above that budget; before this ticket what happened there was
; target-dependent: InvalidAllocation on a 32-bit target (wasm32, where
; the Substrate pallet runs), a sparse allocation on 64-bit Rust, and a
; sparse allocation on xqvm_py's unbounded integers. This vector reaches
; step 3 on every implementation and pins InvalidAllocation everywhere.
;
; size = 4294967296 = 2^32, one past the maximum. Charging it costs
; size * 8 = 34359738368 bytes = 32 GiB, so the vector's budget is set to
; 68719476736 bytes (64 GiB) -- comfortably above the 32 GiB charge, so
; the charge at step 2 succeeds and step 3 is what decides the fault.
; BQMX allocates a sparse model (XqmxModel::new stores size as a number
; and materialises no buffer), so this budget costs no real memory.

PUSH 4294967296
BQMX r0
HALT
